WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:10.030
SANJAY SARMA: The
person who invents

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:15.820
a new sustainable energy source
or figures out how to remove

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.010
plastics from the ocean--

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:20.890
it doesn't matter whether they
have a degree or certificate.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.680
What matters is can they
actually achieve it.

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.410
That, I think, is what we
need to keep our eye on, which

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.590
is, can they solve the problem?

00:00:29.590 --> 00:00:31.090
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:33.340
we're sitting down
with Sanjay Sarma,

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.460
the Vice President for
Open Learning at MIT.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.420
Sanjay wears many hats.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.840
He's a professor of
mechanical engineering,

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.200
a developer, writer,
a parent, and, perhaps

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.950
most salient for us at
MIT OpenCourseWare, Sanjay

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:49.330
is a learner.

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:53.080
In today's episode, Sanjay
talks to us about learning,

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.940
from the social
systems behind how

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:57.880
we learn to the biology
of how we're wired to make

00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.860
sense of the world around us.

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.615
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen.

00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:10.050
In August of 2020, Sanjay
released his book, Grasp:

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:13.680
The Science Transforming How
We Learn, which he co-authored

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.820
with Luke Yoquinto.

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.900
SANJAY SARMA: It's
about how we learn.

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:19.390
It's an attempt
to describe how we

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:22.270
learn with a biographical
aspect to it.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:25.210
Some ask me, why
is it called Grasp?

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:26.115
Why Grasp?

00:01:26.115 --> 00:01:26.740
Think about it.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.490
In English, when you
really get something,

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:30.520
you call it grasping something.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.460
I think the German
word is similar.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.830
Comprehension-- it has
the word "apprehend."

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:37.930
You apprehend someone.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:39.460
It's grabbing.

00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:42.815
In Hindi, which is one of
the languages I grew up with,

00:01:42.815 --> 00:01:44.440
you also use a similar
word, which is--

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:45.520
I really got the concept.

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.830
You grab it.

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.360
So what does it
really mean to learn?

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.570
That's what this book is about.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:54.040
And what is the
science behind it?

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.763
How does curiosity drive it?

00:01:55.763 --> 00:01:57.430
What does it mean to
memorize something?

00:01:57.430 --> 00:01:59.710
What does it mean
to forget something?

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.440
What does it mean when you
actually remember something,

00:02:02.440 --> 00:02:03.895
but you can't access it?

00:02:03.895 --> 00:02:05.770
But when you get it,
once you've accessed it,

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:07.220
it all comes flowing out.

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:09.009
So that's what
this book is about.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:11.050
And it tries to map
it from neurons all

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:15.230
the way to a societal level.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:17.680
SARAH HANSEN: So what prompted
him to write the book?

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.930
For Sanjay, it was
about uncovering

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.360
the history of
schooling and rethinking

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:24.940
the methods we use to teach.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:27.130
SANJAY SARMA: I always
wondered why we ended up

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:28.660
doing things the way we do.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.190
And it turns out
that a lot of it

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:33.700
is happenstance, and perhaps
a historical accident.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:37.210
The impact of what we were
trying to educate people about,

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:39.820
which initially was
religious books--

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.730
and eventually, it was
memorizing aspects of the book,

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:44.290
and then it was the
Greek thread that

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.415
wove into that, and then
the Industrial Revolution,

00:02:46.415 --> 00:02:48.080
and so on.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:50.980
And so the question was, if
you went back to the basics,

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.620
how would you teach and
what would you teach?

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.710
I, too, am a product of
Western education principles.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:57.460
And I did well.

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.210
But I also struggled.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:02.290
And so I started wondering
what the science tells us

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:05.530
about how educational
systems should be designed.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:07.420
So once I started
researching it,

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.820
I thought, oh my god, the
story needs to be told.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:11.590
So the book started
writing itself.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:12.610
And then I found Luke.

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.590
And he shaped it with me.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:17.740
And that's what led to Grasp.

00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:20.200
SARAH HANSEN: So what's the
difference between simply

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.610
picking up concepts and
actually grasping them?

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:27.760
According to Sanjay, what
it often comes down to

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.800
is having an
actionable component

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:34.300
to learning, not just hearing
and regurgitating information,

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:39.140
but locking it in place
by physically applying it.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:41.960
SANJAY SARMA: We
have this assumption

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:46.120
that the learner's mind
is a sheet of paper

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.630
and the professor has a
pen and all the professor

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:52.900
has to do is write on the sheet
of paper and declare victory.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:55.750
And after 10 minutes,
15 minutes, 20 minutes,

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:57.730
if the learner isn't
paying attention,

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:01.330
that's the learner's fault,
not a teacher's fault.

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:04.750
And that is just
fundamentally wrong.

00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:06.830
And every parent knows
this, by the way.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:10.100
When we go to the classroom,
we just ignore that truth.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:15.520
The fact is the learner is
forming a model of the world.

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:18.250
It's like a plant growing.

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:21.519
You can't give the plant a
lifetime's worth of water

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:24.340
on the first day and
say, you're done,

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:28.380
and all the potassium on day 2
and all the nitrogen on day 3--

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:29.650
can't do that.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:31.330
The plant wants
water or sunlight

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.480
or a particular
type of fertilizer

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.480
when the plant wants it.

00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:37.240
By the way, that's called
precision agriculture.

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:38.770
It's a field.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:41.290
But yet we do that
to human beings.

00:04:41.290 --> 00:04:44.470
I want to teach you all the
math you need in the first four

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:45.470
months.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:46.580
You should be done.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:48.370
That's not how it works.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:52.420
It's when you use the math
that it becomes valuable.

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:55.180
And every classroom violates it.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:56.590
Every class violates it.

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:59.200
Curricula violate
that principle.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:01.060
What marks something
as something

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.430
that needs to be
remembered or as valuable?

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:05.350
Well, when you process it.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:06.910
So if you have a
nice conversation

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:10.990
with your child or your
spouse, you remember that.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:16.090
But the conversation you
had, quotidian conversation

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:18.980
about something--

00:05:18.980 --> 00:05:21.215
don't forget to pay the
bill or something-- yeah,

00:05:21.215 --> 00:05:22.090
you'll remember that.

00:05:22.090 --> 00:05:25.270
But the conversation
itself isn't memorable.

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:31.390
So engagement and purpose
flag learning for the brain

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:32.740
as being valuable.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:37.673
And they put it in a sequence
that makes it actionable.

00:05:37.673 --> 00:05:40.090
SARAH HANSEN: I've been working
with Sanjay for many years

00:05:40.090 --> 00:05:41.500
at MIT Open Learning.

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:43.960
And the phrase that
I hear him utter most

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.870
is, how does your idea scale?

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:48.580
I wanted to take
this opportunity

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:51.940
to ask him about his focus
on scaling innovations

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.143
in learning.

00:05:53.143 --> 00:05:55.060
SANJAY SARMA: There's
something about learning

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:00.580
that is a human evolutionary
aspect of our existence, which

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:02.560
is children learn,
parents teach.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:03.380
And we keep that.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:05.950
So as an adult, I
learn in similar ways

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:10.150
to how I learned as a baby
from my parents, as a child,

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:12.430
as a young person.

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:14.710
Parenting is not scalable.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.860
There's a reason we
have fewer children,

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.060
but we give them full attention.

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:22.660
So I think our
biggest challenge is

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:25.030
there are lots of people,
lots of young people,

00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:27.560
lots of adults in the
world that need to learn.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:30.550
And the challenge
is, can we scale

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:33.130
what is very hard to scale?

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:35.740
It's sort of of the
most important question.

00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:38.380
And we can only do it if we
understand how people learn

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:40.870
and understand how
technology could help

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:43.840
and how humans could help
and how coaching could help.

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:47.230
But the scale of
demand is so vast.

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:49.750
If you want to make an impact,
you've got to figure out,

00:06:49.750 --> 00:06:53.210
how do we scale this thing
that is so difficult to scale?

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:57.247
So to me, that's the
existential question.

00:06:57.247 --> 00:06:59.580
SARAH HANSEN: One of the
interesting things about Sanjay

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.610
is that he's helping solve
this question of scalability

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.500
through online learning.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.750
As Sanjay will
explain in a moment,

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:09.480
online learning cuts out
many of the characteristics

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:13.410
of traditional classroom
models, such as one-way lectures

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:16.230
and an emphasis on memorization.

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:18.360
SANJAY SARMA: So imagine
you're in the desert

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:19.950
and you're thirsty.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:23.890
If I give you a glass of
water which is only half-full,

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:26.340
you're not going to say,
oh my god, it's half-empty.

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:28.320
You're going to
say it's half-full.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:30.450
So for those who don't
have access to learning,

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:33.930
online education is
a glass half-full.

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.300
But it's still water.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.640
For those who can have
residential education,

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:41.610
online learning is
quite something else.

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:45.660
It displaces lecture time and
creates room for doing all

00:07:45.660 --> 00:07:50.790
the hands-on stuff that makes
education doubly wonderful--

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:54.210
the curiosity, the coaching,
the field trips and discussions

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:57.000
and disputation, the
fun, the joy of learning,

00:07:57.000 --> 00:07:59.490
the-- what I call
the magic of MIT.

00:07:59.490 --> 00:08:02.370
And online learning
gets rid of the thing

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:06.540
we waste a lot of time on,
which is one-way lectures.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:09.910
So to me, online learning has
two very different purposes.

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:15.090
It's a glass half-full for
those who are parched for water.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:16.980
It's still great water.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:20.550
So online learning--
it is something

00:08:20.550 --> 00:08:23.460
that makes in-person
education much richer,

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:28.350
as well, by leaving time to do
the hands-on stuff that today

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:29.650
is occupied by lectures.

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:31.790
So that's the flipped classroom.

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:34.400
SARAH HANSEN: Something Sanjay
has helped develop at MIT

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:36.980
is the MicroMasters
program, which

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:39.860
makes it possible for people
from all over the world

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:43.039
to earn professional and
academic credentials for MIT

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:45.800
courses they take
online, credentials

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:48.050
that can be applied
toward degrees at MIT

00:08:48.050 --> 00:08:51.342
and other institutions
of higher learning.

00:08:51.342 --> 00:08:52.800
SANJAY SARMA: The
MicroMasters gets

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:55.330
rid of the winnowing function.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:56.260
So let me explain.

00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:58.740
So how does a
master's program work?

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:02.220
You admit someone based on
letters of recommendations

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:04.950
and scores, letters from people
you probably have never met,

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:07.560
scores from universities
you haven't visited

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:09.660
or you don't know.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.550
Admissions-- it's a
winnowing function.

00:09:11.550 --> 00:09:14.430
It's based on such
sparse information.

00:09:14.430 --> 00:09:17.610
What we do with the MicroMasters
is there is no admissions.

00:09:17.610 --> 00:09:19.030
All comers are welcome.

00:09:19.030 --> 00:09:19.770
It's online.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:22.920
The incremental cost of
adding a student is very low.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:23.760
Come on in.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.890
Take the courses.

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:27.110
Finish up.

00:09:27.110 --> 00:09:28.110
Take the proctored exam.

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:28.693
It's not easy.

00:09:28.693 --> 00:09:29.880
It's difficult.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:32.100
When you finish, you
have a MicroMasters.

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.297
And oh, by the way,
if you did really well

00:09:35.297 --> 00:09:37.630
and you get into school, we'll
give you a credit for it.

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:39.970
So we've reversed the funnel.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:43.410
So that's what the
MicroMasters is.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.160
SARAH HANSEN: Sanjay
has been on this path

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:46.930
of making educational
materials more

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:50.750
accessible on a large
scale for over 20 years.

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:52.660
So I asked him
what advice he has

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:55.900
for other educators about
how to engage with Grasp

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:59.380
and how to get involved
with scaling education.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:02.620
SANJAY SARMA: What I suggest
educators do is, first of all,

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:08.230
write down a wish list of
what they would like to do,

00:10:08.230 --> 00:10:11.230
what they would like to see in
the education system, how they

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:14.410
do it differently, and how
it maps to how they taught

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:16.360
their own kids or
how they learned

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:19.330
and how a good
teacher inspired them.

00:10:19.330 --> 00:10:22.270
I was involved a little bit in
the creation of OpenCourseWare

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:23.140
20 years ago.

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:27.740
And when I was doing my dynamics
lectures, a colleague said,

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:30.460
hey, can I make recordings
because I'm picking up

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:32.620
your course and I want
to see how-- the approach

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:34.340
you take to teach it?

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:36.307
And so when I made
the recordings,

00:10:36.307 --> 00:10:38.140
I called up OpenCourseWare
and I said, look,

00:10:38.140 --> 00:10:40.098
if it's useful to you,
I'd like to put them up,

00:10:40.098 --> 00:10:43.370
for what it's worth, because
I teach dynamics differently.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:46.370
And one thing led to another
and it just went up there.

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:52.150
And I actually think that to
un-self-consciously just share

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:55.240
yourself is something-- there's
something liberating about it,

00:10:55.240 --> 00:10:56.410
frankly.

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:58.820
And I recommend that others try.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:00.640
Just make a YouTube
video of yourself

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:01.750
doing something you love.

00:11:01.750 --> 00:11:02.950
Put it up.

00:11:02.950 --> 00:11:06.800
And I'm sure it will
be of value to others.

00:11:06.800 --> 00:11:08.540
SARAH HANSEN: Making
ourselves vulnerable

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:11.630
can lead to something of
great value for others.

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:15.530
It's something we see every
day here at MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:19.430
So we'd like to say a huge thank
you to Sanjay Sarma and all

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:23.060
of the MIT faculty who not only
share their teaching materials,

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:26.060
but also share how they
teach through interviews

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:29.850
on our website and through
podcast episodes like this one.

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:32.750
You can find Sanjay Sarma's
teaching materials on our MIT

00:11:32.750 --> 00:11:34.520
OpenCourseWare website.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:36.560
You can also pick
up a copy of Grasp

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:38.870
at your local
library or bookshop.

00:11:38.870 --> 00:11:41.720
Some other books he suggests
educators pair with Grasp

00:11:41.720 --> 00:11:45.830
are Make It Stick and Peak:
The Science of Expertise.

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:47.660
And be sure to check
out Sanjay's latest

00:11:47.660 --> 00:11:52.820
book, Workforce Education: A
New Roadmap, from MIT Press.

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.570
Thank you so much for listening.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:58.020
Until next time, signing off
from Cambridge, Massachusetts,

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:03.080
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen,
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:06.410
Chalk Radio's producers
include myself, Brett Paci

